      <h1>Why Systems?</h1>

      <p>
        We live in a world obsessed with events.
      </p>

      <p>
        A product launch fails. An AI model hallucinates. A team misses its
        goals. A company loses momentum. A promising career stalls.
      </p>

      <p>
        Our instinct is to investigate the event itself. We ask what happened,
        who made the mistake, or which decision triggered the outcome.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        But events are only the visible surface of much deeper systems.
      </blockquote>

      <p>
        Every outcome is shaped by something larger: incentives, structures,
        feedback loops, constraints, culture, architecture, policies, and the
        countless interactions between them.
      </p>

      <p>
        When we only fix symptoms, the same patterns return. When we understand
        the system, we create the possibility for lasting change.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p>
        Systems thinking doesn't provide easy answers.
      </p>

      <p>
        It asks better questions.
      </p>

      <blockquote>
        What conditions made this outcome likely?
        <br /><br />
        What incentives are driving this behavior?
        <br /><br />
        Which feedback loops are reinforcing the problem?
        <br /><br />
        How might we redesign the system instead of reacting to its symptoms?
      </blockquote>

      <hr />

      <p>
        This perspective applies everywhere.
      </p>

      <p>
        In AI, it helps us think beyond models to governance, trust,
        observability, and human oversight.
      </p>

      <p>
        In engineering, it shifts our attention from individual features to
        architecture, reliability, and long-term evolution.
      </p>

      <p>
        In organizations, it reveals how incentives and structures shape
        behavior more than intentions.
      </p>

      <p>
        In careers, it encourages us to design environments that compound
        learning rather than simply accumulating experience.
      </p>

      <hr />

      <p>
        84Hz exists to explore these ideas through essays, research, mental
        models, and practical frameworks.
      </p>

      <p class="closing">
        Because changing outcomes begins with changing the systems that produce
        them.
      </p>

    </article>
